# Where to resume after a subtask failed its verification.
#
# A failed manipulation is retried by re-approaching, never by repeating the
# manipulation from where it broke -- that is what the operators did in all
# 92 recovered failures in the dataset. Failures at the tray resume at the
# tray (approach_pick, pick, place) with no `move` in between, because that
# is the recovery the demonstrations actually contain; planning a `move`
# there leaves the runtime waiting for a subtask nobody performs.
#
# The retry budget is the manager's, not the planner's. This answers only
# "what sequence comes next", so a planner swap cannot quietly change how
# many times the cell will re-grab a part.

string goal

# Object state the current plan was made for, as MakePlan returned it.
string state

# Neutral goal sentence from the current plan, carried through unchanged.
string task_prompt

# The plan as it stands, starting with the subtask that was running. Needed
# because a tray retry is recognised by its shape: a `pick` whose remaining
# plan still has `place` but no `move` is itself already a tray retry.
string[] current_subtasks

# Subtask that did not achieve its goal.
string failed_subtask
---
bool success
string reason

# Sequence to resume with, starting with the subtask to run now.
string[] subtasks
